INSERT INTO blogs (title, description, imageUrl, para1, para2, para3) VALUES
('How to plan a college fest without losing your mind', 'What we learned from running fests for colleges across the city, from sponsors to the last band on stage.', 'about1.png', 'Most college fests start the same way. A big idea in the first committee meeting, a long list of events, and about six weeks to pull it off. The first thing we tell any fest team is to lock three things early: the dates, the main venue on campus and the budget. Everything else depends on these, and changing any of them later costs a lot more than people expect.', 'Sponsors are where most teams struggle. Instead of sending the same deck to fifty companies, pick ten local brands that actually want to reach students, like cafes, coaching institutes, bike rentals and food delivery apps. Offer them something concrete, like a stall, their logo on the entry passes or a mention between performances. A small sponsor who shows up is worth more than a big one who never replies.', 'On the day itself, keep one person free whose only job is to handle problems. The sound check will run late, a judge will cancel and someone will lose the keys to the green room. If that one person is not also running an event, these things get fixed in minutes instead of turning into a crisis. And please keep water and first aid near the main stage, especially for the pro-night.'),
('Choosing a wedding venue: what to ask before you pay the advance', 'The banquet hall looks perfect on the visit. Here is what to ask before you sign anything.', 'services2.png', 'A venue visit is usually planned to impress you. The lights are on, the hall is decorated from the last function and the manager is very friendly. Before you pay the advance, ask how many guests the hall can seat for a sit-down dinner, not just how many people fit standing. For a wedding with 400 guests, those two numbers can be very different.', 'Next, ask what is included in the per-plate rate and what is extra. Decoration, DJ, generator backup, valet parking and the bridal room are often charged separately. Ask whether you must use their caterer and decorator or can bring your own, and if outside vendors have to pay an entry fee. Also check the timing rules. Many venues in the city have to stop music by 10 PM, which matters a lot if you are planning a sangeet.', 'Finally, get the cancellation and date change policy in writing. Dates shift for many reasons, and you do not want to lose the full advance because of a muhurat change. If the venue hesitates to put something in the contract, treat that as your answer. A good venue manager will be happy to write it down.'),
('Corporate events that people actually want to attend', 'Annual meets, product launches and offsites do not have to feel like a compulsory meeting with snacks.', 'about2.png', 'The biggest mistake in corporate events is packing the agenda. Six back-to-back presentations after lunch will lose the room, no matter how good the speakers are. We usually suggest keeping talks under 20 minutes, adding a short break every hour and putting the most important session in the morning when people are still fresh.', 'Give people a reason to talk to each other. Mix seating across teams, plan one activity that needs groups to work together, and keep the dinner relaxed instead of formal. For product launches, a live demo almost always works better than slides. Let people touch the product and ask questions.', 'After the event, send a short feedback form the next day while people still remember it. Three or four questions are enough. We use the answers to plan the next event, and companies are often surprised by how honest and useful the replies are.'),
('Making an event budget that does not fall apart', 'A simple way to plan your spending so the last week before the event is not full of surprises.', 'services1.png', 'Start by writing down every item you will pay for, even the small ones. Venue, food, decor, sound and lights, photography, invitations, transport, permissions and staff. People usually remember the big costs and forget things like generator fuel, extra chairs or tips for the staff, and these add up quickly.', 'Once you have the list, split it into must-haves and nice-to-haves. If the budget is tight, the nice-to-haves are the first to go. Then keep 10 to 15 percent of the total aside as a buffer. Something always costs more than the quote, especially if the guest count goes up in the last two weeks.', 'Track every payment in one place, whether that is a notebook or a shared spreadsheet. Note who was paid, how much, and what is still pending. When three family members or two departments are all paying vendors, this is the only way to avoid paying someone twice or forgetting a balance until the day of the event.'),
('Promoting your event on social media', 'How to get people to actually show up, not just like your posts.', 'services3.png', 'Start posting at least three weeks before the event. The first post should answer the basic questions clearly: what it is, when, where and how to register. Keep the date and venue on every post after that too. Most people see only one or two of your posts, so each one should make sense on its own.', 'Short videos work much better than posters. A 15 second clip of last year''s event, the venue being set up or a performer saying they are coming will get more shares than any designed graphic. Ask your speakers, artists and sponsors to share the posts from their own accounts as well. Their followers are often exactly the people you want.', 'In the last week, focus on reminders and urgency. Post how many seats are left, share the schedule and answer questions in the comments. Send a reminder to registered people one day before with the time, location and a map link. Fewer people forget, and fewer people get lost on the way.');
